  },
  "articleBody": "Ever installed some built-in shelves only to find them just a bit… off-kilter? You're not alone! A lot of folks run into common mistakes that can throw off the whole look and function of their new shelving. One big one is not fully accounting for wall irregularities. Walls are rarely perfectly flat or plumb, and if you don't shim and level carefully during installation, those slight imperfections will show. Another frequent issue is not properly securing the shelves into studs, especially for heavier items. If you're just screwing into drywall, you're setting yourself up for sagging or even collapse. Sometimes, people underestimate how deep a shelf needs to be or forget about crucial clearances, leading to awkward-looking or unusable spaces. Skipping the final leveling and shimming before permanent fastening is also a recipe for disaster; that's your last chance to make everything perfect. Not taking the time to dry-fit everything before the final install can also lead to surprises. If you want a truly seamless, professional look, especially with custom or multi-unit setups, it's often best to call in an expert. They’ve got the tools and experience to handle those tricky wall conditions and ensure everything is perfectly plumb, level, and secure.",
